                        "review": "At its most basic level, you could call A Sky Full of Stars a dating game. After all, the four routes involve the main character, Akito, choosing one of four girls to date. But if that’s all this game was, I’d never have picked it up.\r\n\r\nThe characters, not only Akito and the girls, but the others you meet through the 6 Stars Club, are all well-fleshed out and I loved reading every moment of these stories. The relationships felt real and all had their problems that the characters needed to work through. Not to mention that each route involves a different big star event, and an after credits future scene.\r\n\r\nThere are two choice points in this game. The first will decide if you go down the Saya/Hikari path or the Orihime/Korona path, and the second choice will decide which of those two remaining girls you choose.\r\n\r\nBut the heart of this story was definitely a love of the stars. These characters all loved staring up at the night sky, learning and experiencing, and I came to love it just as much as they did. There was talk of stars, constellations, myths, and meteor showers, and the ever present wish to bring back the bright night of years ago.\r\n\r\nI cannot express how much I loved this visual novel. I was so sad to come to the end, to have to leave these characters. It really imbedded in me a deep desire to look at the stars myself. The soundtrack was also superb and I’m so glad that I purchased it with the game. I would absolutely recommend A Sky Full of Stars to someone looking for a great story.\r\n\r\nRoutes ranking: Saya>Orihime=Korona>Hikari",
